 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Splatting for Anti-aliasing Small Cells](829-splatting-for-anti-aliasing-small-cells.md)
-Prevents loss of geometric detail in adaptive mesh volume rendering by replacing small, easily missed polyhedral cells with anti-aliased ellipsoidal splats. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Threshold Slicing and Anti-Aliasing](1447-threshold-slicing-and-anti-aliasing.md) by explicitly using ellipsoidal splats convolved with a Gaussian presampling filter to handle the anti-aliasing, rather than relying solely on polygon rasterization or post-processing blurs.
+Prevents loss of geometric detail in adaptive mesh volume rendering by replacing small, easily missed polyhedral cells with anti-aliased ellipsoidal splats. It sets itself apart by explicitly using ellipsoidal splats convolved with a Gaussian presampling filter to handle anti-aliasing, rather than relying solely on polygon rasterization. Choose this when rendering unstructured or curvilinear grids with significant cell size variation.
 
 ### [Threshold Slicing and Anti-Aliasing](1447-threshold-slicing-and-anti-aliasing.md)
-A hybrid technique for curvilinear and unstructured grids that partitions cells based on a scalar value threshold to balance geometric precision and rendering performance. It differs from pure polygonal slicing by handling inter-slice gaps and low-density regions via splatting, and from pure splatting by providing exact boundaries for significant features.
+A hybrid technique for curvilinear and unstructured grids that partitions cells based on a scalar value threshold to balance geometric precision and performance. It solves aliasing in large datasets by rendering high-value cells as exact projected polygons and low-value cells as anti-aliased ellipsoidal splats. Choose this when exact boundaries for significant features are required alongside smooth representation of low-density regions.
 
 ### [Anti-Aliasing for Splatting](4471-anti-aliasing-for-splatting.md)
-Prevents sample-rate aliasing artifacts during the resampling phase by dynamically scaling splat size and attenuating energy for splats beyond a critical distance $k$, thereby maintaining constant energy per unit area on the view plane and satisfying the Nyquist criterion for the integration grid. Unlike volume ray-casting, which performs reconstruction in eye space and naturally adapts kernel size as rays diverge, splatting performs reconstruction in perspective space and requires this explicit mechanism to avoid aliasing.
+Prevents sample-rate aliasing artifacts during resampling by dynamically scaling splat size and attenuating energy for splats beyond a critical distance $k$. It distinguishes itself by maintaining constant energy per unit area on the view plane and satisfying the Nyquist criterion for the integration grid. Choose this for standard volume rasters where perspective projection causes sampling rate mismatches at depth.
 
 ## Background
-Not given in the sources.
-## Sources+The problem of aliasing in volume rendering, particularly during resampling, was formally addressed in the context of splatting by Westover . The distinction between eye-space reconstruction (ray casting) and perspective-space reconstruction (splatting) highlights the unique challenges of maintaining the Nyquist criterion in splatting-based pipelines . Subsequent developments introduced hybrid approaches combining polygonal slicing with splatting to handle unstructured grids and adaptive meshes, such as those by Kindlmann and Westover  and later refinements by others .
